{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Attention.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Attention.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"attention attention","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Attention.md","fileName":"Attention.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Attention","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Human Cognition]] [[Player Experience Layers]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human Cognition","Player Experience Layers","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Attention.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Attention.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"attention attention","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Attention.md","fileName":"Attention.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Attention","heading":"Attention","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Attention selects some information for further processing while inhibiting other information. It includes orienting to sensory events, detecting signals for focused processing, and maintaining alertness.\n\nSelective attention chooses what to process, focused attention maintains processing despite distraction, and divided attention attempts to process more than one event at once. Moving attention requires disengaging from one location, shifting, and reengaging elsewhere.\n\nAttention is limited. Overload, peripheral vision limits, rapid events, or divided tasks can cause missed changes and errors. An interface can direct attention toward urgent information, but competing cues may also distract from the primary task.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human Cognition","Player Experience Layers","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Attention.md::2